INT. FORMER BOWLING ALLEY - EVIDENCE ANNEX - NIGHT

Lane 12 is a ghost of itself. Waxed maple, gutters stacked with cardboard banker's boxes. Overhead, a dead scoreboard still reads HOME 114 / GUEST 98.

DET. ADAEZE OKONKWO, 40s, rain-wet trench coat, reading glasses pushed up into close-cropped hair, walks the lane like she owns the lease.

At the ball return sits SGT. HALVARD PEDERSEN, 60, heavy, a thermos between his knees, pretending not to be asleep.

OKONKWO
Who signed the boxes out?

PEDERSEN
Nobody. That's the problem.

A TECH in a paper suit kneels at the far end, photographing a box lid with a ring light. Young, narrow shoulders, earbuds in one ear only.

OKONKWO
You. What's your name?

TECH
Just Tech tonight. Ask the lab.

She moves on. Doesn't push.

By the shoe-rental counter, MRS. KOWALCZYK, 70s, cardigan buttoned wrong, clutches a handbag like a flotation device. She owned this alley for thirty years.

MRS. KOWALCZYK
Strike night was Thursdays. Forty-one lanes of noise. Now listen to it.

Silence. A compressor hums somewhere.

OKONKWO
Ma'am, did you give anyone a key?

MRS. KOWALCZYK
The city has my keys. The city has everything.

PEDERSEN
(into thermos)
Ain't that the truth.

OFFICER TRAN, 20s, stiff, enters from the lobby with a clipboard. He stops at the edge of the lane, unsure if he's allowed to step on it.

OFFICER TRAN
Detective. Gate log's clean. Last entry, six forty p.m. Someone named Lennox.

OKONKWO
First name?

OFFICER TRAN
Just Lennox.

Behind a stack of boxes, a cough. Everyone looks.

LENNOX, 50s, gray ponytail, security lanyard, stands up slowly from where he's been sitting on a crate, hands visible and empty.

LENNOX
I'm the night man. I was in the office.

OKONKWO
Mr. Lennox. You've been in the building the whole time.

LENNOX
It's a warm building.

The Tech glances up, finally pulls the earbud out.

TECH
Bashir. It's Bashir. Since you asked.

OKONKWO
Thank you, Bashir.

BASHIR
Box nineteen has been opened. Seal's cut clean. Razor, not a knife.

OKONKWO
How do you know?

BASHIR
Edge doesn't tear. I do this for a living.

Okonkwo takes off her glasses. Folds them. Looks at Lennox for a long, unhurried moment.

OKONKWO
Walk me through the office, Mr. Lennox.

LENNOX
Sure.

He doesn't move. Neither does she.

PEDERSEN
I'll get the coffee.

He doesn't move either.

BASHIR
Light's good here, if anyone wants to take their time.

Okonkwo sits down on the foul line, crosses her legs, and waits.
